    "<font color='darkred'> Unless otherwise noted, **this notebook will not be reviewed or autograded.**</font> You are welcome to use it for scratchwork, but **only the files listed in the exercises will be checked.**\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# Streamlit Preview\n",
    "\n",
    "You'll notice that this repository contains an *app.py* file and an *apputil.py* file. The *app.py* file can be used to run a [Streamlit](https://streamlit.io/) app, and the *apputil.py* file contains utility functions that may be used by the app. Editing the *app.py* file is usually optional, and **you will typically add your exercise code to the *apputil.py* file.**\n",
    "\n",
    "We will explore Streamlit in more detail later, but you are encouraged to try it out on your own every now and then! The best way to learn is by doing, and Streamlit is a great tool for quickly building interactive applications in Python.\n",
    "\n",
    "To start, try editing the *app.py* file, and run it with the command `streamlit run app.py`. You should see a web page open up in your browser with the Streamlit app running. You can make changes to the *app.py* file and see the results in real-time.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Note: there is a lot in [the Streamlit documentation](https://docs.streamlit.io/get-started/fundamentals/main-concepts) that we will cover later on, so for now just _play around with it in the same way you will with Python in general!_**"
   ]
